layout: base.njk
title: Book a consultation
description: Booking is by email. Send Sarah a short message and she'll reply to arrange a time that suits you.
---

{#- Screen 2d, minus the Calendly. The mockup predates the briefing's decision
    that booking is by email — see "Pages in scope" and section 5, and the note
    saying the briefing wins. So: layout and type from 2d, content from the
    briefing. No Calendly button, no "opens in a new tab" caption, and no
    "Prefer email?" block — it was the fallback under that button, and email is
    now the page itself.

    No permalink and no contactFooter, as on 2b and 2c: this file maps to /book/,
    which the nav and the buttons on 2b and 2c already point at, and the compact
dark footer comes from leaving contactFooter unset. -#}
{#- PROPOSED COPY, awaiting your sign-off. The h1 is the design's own, verbatim;
    the lead and the three steps are new, because what they replace was about
    Calendly. Two facts are reused rather than invented: "free 20-minute intro
    call" from 2a and 2c, "within one working day" from footer.njk. -#}
{% set steps = [
  "You email Sarah — a couple of lines about where you are is plenty",
  "She replies within one working day with a few times",
  "You pick one, and that's your free 20-minute intro call"
] %}
<div class="page-head">
  <h1 class="page-head__title">Book a consultation</h1>
  <p class="page-head__lead">
    Booking is by email. Send Sarah a message and she'll reply to arrange a time that suits you.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="book-panel">
  {#- An ol where the design has plain divs — three steps in a fixed order are an
      ordered list, and saying so gets "list, 3 items" announced first. The
      numbers are spans rather than ::marker because the design gives them a
      colour, a weight and a 16px column, and a span is the duller way to get all
      three. No aria-hidden: like the badge on 2c they are information. -#}
  <ol class="book-steps">
    {%- for step in steps %}
      <li class="book-step">
        <span class="book-step__number">{{ loop.index }}</span>{{ step }}
      </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
  {#- TODO-SARAH: the dedicated email address — briefing section 6, question 4.
      Two lines change when we have it and nothing else on this page: this href
      becomes mailto:ADDRESS?subject=Consultation%20enquiry, and the span below
      becomes the address.

      The href is deliberately not a URL, so it 404s and the marker survives into
      _site — the convention index.njk uses. Specifically not
      mailto:TODO-SARAH-…, which is a valid mailto: and would open a compose
      window addressed to nonsense: exactly the silent failure this guards
      against. subject= and no body=: it labels the enquiry in Sarah's inbox,
      stays plain ASCII so the encoding is legible, and puts no words in the
      visitor's mouth. -#}
  <a class="button" href="TODO-SARAH-email-address">Email Sarah to book</a>
  {#- 2d's "Opens calendly.com in a new tab" slot, now load-bearing. A mailto: on
      a device with no mail client does nothing visible, so the address must be
      readable, selectable text too. One sentence says what the button does and
      names the address, so neither element repeats the other, and it sits under
      the thumb at the moment the button failed.

      Plain text, not a second mailto:, which would give a screen reader two
      adjacent links to the same place. Its type is the one deliberate departure
      from the mockup: 2d's caption is 12.5px in --color-text-caption, ~2.6:1 on
      this grey — fine for a throwaway note, not for the one string a visitor may
      have to read off the screen and type elsewhere. -#}
  <p class="book-panel__address">
    Opens your email app, addressed to <span class="book-panel__email">TODO-SARAH — email address</span>
  </p>
</div>
